in the cold darkness of the early  morning tens of thousands of devotees  trekked to the hilltop temple of mata  vaishnodevi an indian-administered  kashmir  at about 3 am there was a surge in the  crowd witnesses describe a horrifying  crush and people trampling over bodies  it's a tragedy many people are dead  initial reports suggest an altercation  between groups visiting the shrine  perhaps to offer special prayers for the  new year but security has also been  called into question  the police were very irresponsible they  did not put up dividers in the area  outside the cave they mixed also those  coming out of the shrine and those going  in i saw it with my own eyes police  pushed the pilgrims and also waved  battens and blew whistles to intimidate  them which spread panic and there was a  stampede in no time  mata vaishna devi shrine is one of  india's most revered hindu sites before  the pandemic about a hundred thousand  devotees would climb the steep winding  track every day to the narrow cave  containing the shrine  authorities had kept the daily number at  25 000 but witnesses suggest many more  people were there  somewhere there was mismanagement when  we knew that there was this type of rush  we should have stopped the public first  if we'd stopped them earlier this  tragedy would not have happened  an investigation is underway into what  sparked the crush while the injured and  families of victims have been offered  compensation  deadly stampedes are fairly common  during indian religious festivals as  large crowds gather in small areas often  with few safety or crowd control  measures  [Music]  i express my condolences to the families  who have lost their dear ones and the  sad incident of the stampede my  sympathies to those who are injured  access to the shrine was halted for  several hours but later resumed  alexi o'brien al jazeera
 

